WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
ChatGPT just learned how to look at every tab you have open and actually do things for you. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 199. Open ai added fresh abilities to its add-on for the Chrome browser, the decoder reports. That add-on is a tiny extra tool you install so ChatGPT can work inside the pages you already have open. It can read the words on those pages and boil them down into a short recap. It can type into forms for you, the way you fill a school permission slip or

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.100
a checkout page. It can line up products from several shopping pages at once, so you

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.400
are not hopping back and forth. Picture a pile of sticky notes covering your desk.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:44.640
Each one a different website. Now picture a super organized study partner who can glance

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.840
at every note in one look, then write the comparison on a single sheet. That is the shift

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:55.720
here. Chat GPT is no longer only a chat box. It can sit beside your homework or your shopping

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.540
list and handle the tedious clicking.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.440
Free accounts and paid accounts both get these abilities right away.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.780
You do not need a special tier to start.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.820
For a research project, that means less copying sentences from tab to tab by hand.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.940
For online shopping, that means the comparing and organizing can happen in one place instead

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.640
of across a messy row of windows.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.700
There is a real privacy catch and it matters.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.540
The system is looking at whatever you left open, so treat it like inviting someone to

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:24.320
sit at your desk.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.700
close anything personal first, the same way you would close a diary before a helper sits down.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:36.500
If you want to try it, open Chrome and search the Chrome Web Store for the official chat GPT add-on from OpenAI.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.320
Install it, then ask it to recap or compare what is sitting in your open tabs.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.080
You type the request in plain language, the way you already talked to chat GPT,

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.780
and it handles the rest inside the browser window.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.920
Computer use is the name OpenAI gives that hands-on behavior inside work mode.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.220
Work mode is simply a setting where chat GPT is allowed to take actions, not just talk.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.540
Playing a video game is the easiest way to see the steps.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.840
First you look at the screen and notice what is there, like a health bar, a door, or a chest.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:12.120
Second you decide the next move, such as walk forward or pick up the item.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.160
Third you press the button and the game world changes.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.120
4th, you look again and decide the next press.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.440
Computer use follows that same loop on a web page.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.560
The model gets a view of the open tab, the way you stare at a game screen.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:30.760
It chooses an action like click this box or type that address, then it actually types

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.580
or clicks inside the browser and checks what appeared.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:38.240
Filling a form is like watching someone complete a worksheet while you still hold the pencil.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:43.300
You ask for the answers, the helper writes them in the blanks, line by line, and you can

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:44.800
and stop it if a blank looks wrong.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.280
Comparing three product pages is like a personal shopper

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.500
who can stand in three stores at the same time.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.760
One page has the price, another has the shipping time,

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.580
another has the reviews, and the helper pulls

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.060
those pieces onto one list.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.260
None of that requires you to write code.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.300
You just say what you want done in ordinary words.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.200
Once you have seen that loop, the whole idea clicks.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.840
It is looking, choosing, acting, then looking again.

00:03:10.861 --> 00:03:12.741
Google just made the Gmail search bar

00:03:12.741 --> 00:03:18.041
are a lot smarter for people on its artificial intelligence plans, or AI plans, and gadget

00:03:18.041 --> 00:03:18.421
reports.

00:03:19.201 --> 00:03:23.981
AI here just means software that can understand messy human language instead of only exact

00:03:23.981 --> 00:03:24.521
matches.

00:03:25.201 --> 00:03:29.881
Old Gmail search was like a huge control F, the fine shortcut in a document.

00:03:30.401 --> 00:03:32.441
You type a precise word and hope it appears.

00:03:32.981 --> 00:03:37.981
If you cannot remember the exact subject line, that kind of search often fails and the important

00:03:37.981 --> 00:03:39.121
message stays buried.

00:03:39.601 --> 00:03:42.021
The new bar works more like asking a librarian.

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:47.041
You describe what you mean and it hunts by intent, not only by the letters you typed.

00:03:47.421 --> 00:03:51.801
That helps anyone whose inbox is stuffed with hundreds of old emails and zero memory

00:03:51.801 --> 00:03:53.161
of where one message went.

00:03:53.721 --> 00:03:55.841
The search starts to feel like a short conversation.

00:03:56.581 --> 00:04:00.001
You talk, it interprets, then it brings back the mail that fits.

00:04:00.521 --> 00:04:05.221
Here is a free-feeling experiment if a Google AI subscription is already active on the account.

00:04:05.821 --> 00:04:10.581
Open Gmail on your phone or computer and type, find the email about the school trip last

00:04:10.581 --> 00:04:10.961
month.

00:04:10.961 --> 00:04:16.101
Watch whether it surfaces the right thread, even if those exact words never appeared in the subject.

00:04:16.601 --> 00:04:21.681
If you are under 18, you may need a parent to switch that subscription on before the live search appears.

00:04:22.281 --> 00:04:25.241
The point is to cut the frustration of keyword hunt and peck.

00:04:25.641 --> 00:04:28.501
The thing that so often misses the message you actually need.

00:04:29.388 --> 00:04:34.328
A Stanford student used a generative AI model to draft genetic blueprints for tiny viruses.

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:37.408
An MIT technology review sat down with him.

00:04:37.408 --> 00:04:42.668
Generative means the system creates new output from patterns it already learned, the way a

00:04:42.668 --> 00:04:44.888
drawing app invents a picture from a description.

00:04:45.568 --> 00:04:50.628
The talk explores how close we are to AI proposing new life forms and what safety questions

00:04:50.628 --> 00:04:51.228
that raises.

00:04:51.828 --> 00:04:56.308
His work is an early step where software can suggest structures that might one day be built

00:04:56.308 --> 00:04:56.908
in a lab.

00:04:57.468 --> 00:04:59.868
No actual living virus has been created yet.

00:05:00.862 --> 00:05:05.182
One writer on Reddit asks whether we should worry that AI will steal our sense of taste.

00:05:05.182 --> 00:05:10.262
The argument is that these models lean toward the familiar because they trained on work

00:05:10.262 --> 00:05:11.482
that already exists.

00:05:11.942 --> 00:05:16.202
Think of a cover band that can play every hit on the radio while you still get bored

00:05:16.202 --> 00:05:18.402
and hunt for a song nobody has posted yet.

00:05:18.982 --> 00:05:24.102
Humans keep craving surprise and research on how brains react to new things as part of that

00:05:24.102 --> 00:05:24.422
case.

00:05:25.022 --> 00:05:29.642
People naturally move on from whatever becomes common, which could keep human creators

00:05:29.642 --> 00:05:32.182
one step ahead in music, writing, and drawing.

